{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1018456-mexican-moros","id":1018456,"title":"Mexican Moros","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness","Mage: The Awakening"],"posts":[{"date":"2016-12-17T07:55:30-08:00","id":1018456,"title":"Mexican Moros","content":"So I have a player who's character is going to be of Mexican decent, and is going Morose. He's decided that his character has always been interested in death, as his culture is somewhat steeped in it.<br /> <br /> I'm looking for advice on how to bring in traditional Mexican imagery into his awakening. My knowledge on the matter is sadly limited to Mass Media currently (though I will be doing a little research on my own), but my understanding is that their version of death is quite vibrant and, well, lively, which seems at odds with the awakening for Moros. <br /> <br /> Any suggestions would be appreciated!","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11839-cofdave","name":"CofDave","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"31"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-12-17T08:47:12-08:00","id":1018468,"title":"","content":"I don't know a lot about Mexican culture and how it relates to death, but I do know that there's nothing contradictory with it and the way that Moros view Death.<br /> <br /> Have you read the Moros Awakening in the book? Right in the first paragraph it talks about how the Moros has always carried the leaden coin with them and they pay it to cross into Awakening. That to me sounds like they're dropping a heavy burden, in this case the fear of Death. Moros come to understand that Death is just another transitory state that everything must cycle through even if they don't know what's on the other side. This understanding could totally lead to a more celebratory view of death.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2632-mrmdubois","name":"Mrmdubois","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6827"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-12-17T09:03:09-08:00","id":1018474,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Mrmdubois</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1018456-mexican-moros?p=1018468#post1018468\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I don't know a lot about Mexican culture and how it relates to death, but I do know that there's nothing contradictory with it and the way that Moros view Death.<br /> <br /> Have you read the Moros Awakening in the book? Right in the first paragraph it talks about how the Moros has always carried the leaden coin with them and they pay it to cross into Awakening. That to me sounds like they're dropping a heavy burden, in this case the fear of Death. Moros come to understand that Death is just another transitory state that everything must cycle through even if they don't know what's on the other side. This understanding could totally lead to a more celebratory view of death.</div> </div> </div> </div>The Moros awakening specifically calls out how everything is muted. The Mexican after life is always portrayed as being bright and loud.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11839-cofdave","name":"CofDave","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"31"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-12-17T11:19:32-08:00","id":1018517,"title":"","content":"Don't use the book-provided one. There's no reason to do it when you already have good ideas for how to portray an Awakening. Take the book-provided ones as examples and guidelines; go off the rails when they no longer suit.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>I call the Integrity-analogue the &quot;<a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/822559-trying-to-understand-the-morality-system?p=825008#post825008\" target=\"_blank\">subjective stat</a>&quot;.<br /> An explanation how to <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/913221-using-social-manuevering\" target=\"_blank\">use Social Manuevering</a>.<br /> Guanxi Explanations: <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/787518-the-mysterium-and-their-contribution-to-mage-society?p=787824#post787824\" target=\"_blank\">1</a>, <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/787518-the-mysterium-and-their-contribution-to-mage-society?p=788173#post788173\" target=\"_blank\">2</a>, <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/833863-mages-and-trading-favors?p=834531#post834531\" target=\"_blank\">3</a>.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2758-errol216","name":"Errol216","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2758_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1112"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-12-17T11:33:33-08:00","id":1018525,"title":"","content":"Not everyone sees the Supernal the same way, though, is the thing. It's always framed by culture and personal perspective. One of the big stepping stones for a Mage is when they start to realize that their own perspective informs their approach to the Supernal and what they see is merely their limited human mind trying to grasp the grandest cosmic Truths. To borrow a concept, the act of observation changes the outcome.<br /> <br /> Still, that aside, it must be remembered that there is a lot of colour and noise in the modern syncretic echoes, but the Mexica still saw the underworld as shrouded in darkness and shadows. You got closer to Mictlan by descending into lightless caves; getting as deep as you could, where all light and sound from the world is cut off. There's plenty of room to borrow shadows there.<br /> <br /> Don't be afraid to syncretize and use symbolism as you see fit. The point of Mage isn't to provide an authentic itinerary of mythology; it's to tune that symbolism to whatever feels right to you and your player(s). Sleeper versions are the flawed ones. If something in the stories doesn't make sense or work for you or your player(s), change it.<br /> <br /> Remember that the raucous celebration that is found in so many memento moris are reminder that you will die too, one day, and therefore you should live your life well. Some such reminders are sober, but many are incitements to laugh and celebrate. Party now, because it might be your last chance. The dead don't <i>get</i> to celebrate, so the living do it for them; the dead don't get pretty flowers, so the living gather them in offerings.<br /> <br /> There's also the reminder that life comes from death. Like Quetzlcoatl's battle with the Lord of Mictlan to get the bones that humanity sprang from, death is a source of renewal and regeneration in this symbolism. Your ancestors, who are dead, made you, who is alive. One day, you will die, but the things you make will live on. Rot gives way to new growth. Life is a transitory, temporary thing and you can't escape that, so you might as well accept it.<br /> <br /> The character's Mystery Play might honestly be an attempt to bring such life to the underworld; to descend into Mictlan and bring light and colour to the dead whose bodies are rotting and who are surrounded with spiders and owls and shrieking winds; for whom light and warmth are but distant memories and whose eternities are dark, cold, and still. They might hear whispers up from the ground of some departed friend or relative, weeping for the loss of the joys they remembered in life. When the curiosity is too much, they descend into the underworld with garlands of flowers and a basket full of the finest foods, and dare to cross the waters of that bleak reflection of Texcoco to the city of the damned. There, they face a thousand-thousand dead souls, but not simple ghosts or shades; these are skeletons and rotting corpses who look despondently on at the figure among them who, unlike them, still has life. They cannot hope to steal it, but some may lash out in jealous rage. At every step of the way, the dead beg and plead for the wandering soul to take them out of the darkness, or to deliver messages to family, or, more importantly, to give them the contents of their basket. Refusal might be risky or heartbreaking, but they've got to find their friend.<br /> <br /> The Mystery Play ends when the character finally finds their friend and offers the gifts they brought; garlands of flowers and fine foods. And when they give the gifts, colour blooms in the underworld. It spreads like a raging flame. Rotten flesh becomes whole again. Cold winds become warm breezes. The dead take part in a celebration, joyous that they are still, in a sense, alive; alive in the memories and lives of their descendants. Their own deaths are given colour and beauty by defining what their life was lived for. And at the heart of it all, there stands the spectre who gave them passage across the lake, demanding payment at last. The character gives the leaden coin that was in their pocket - a black thing, heavy with the grief that brought them into this dark place - and signs their name in the book of passage, before being directed to the way out: A long climb up the dead tree that sits at the heart of the city, its leaves now blooming into colour, up to where the ceiling of the great cave splits and the character may at last climb to the surface.<br /> <br /> They merge coated in dirt and dust and ash and their soul renewed by the knowledge that death need no longer be feared, so long as one's deeds remain. They are a Moros, now and forevermore, building their mansion in the afterlife with every work a brick.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":6}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2855-axelgear","name":"Axelgear","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2855_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1450"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-12-17T11:51:33-08:00","id":1018531,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>CofDave</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1018456-mexican-moros?p=1018474#post1018474\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The Moros awakening specifically calls out how everything is muted. The Mexican after life is always portrayed as being bright and loud.</div> </div> </div> </div>Stygia is the realm that Moros draw their power from, and it is -an- afterlife, not -the- afterlife. Heck, if you used the Free Council Techne you could totally use Mexican death celebrations to empower your magic which ought to point out to any Moros that the afterlife isn't necessarily all doom and gloom. If you read the books then you'll also see things like Arcadia used to not be called that and the realm of Fate and Time used to be associated with more fatalistic Greek mythology, but it all got changed by Merlin. So that means the Supernal is mutable, or at least a Path's symbols of association with a realm can change. This means a Moros might deliberately set out to change the association of the Path of Doom from Stygia to whatever the Mexican afterlife is, obviously accomplishing that would be an archmaster level undertaking, but there's nothing stopping you from playing with the seed of that ambition in a Mage game. Maybe the Mage doesn't actually want to change Stygia, but he does want to play against the stereotype of being a Moros so he plays up his magical associations with death to be bright and loud instead of dark and dour. Path Tools play into this for you too if you come at it from the right angle.<br /> <br /> Basically, you can totally play a celebratory Death mage with a Mexican flair. With a little imaginative effort it's totally justifiable.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2632-mrmdubois","name":"Mrmdubois","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6827"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-12-17T11:55:34-08:00","id":1018533,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Axelgear</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1018456-mexican-moros?p=1018525#post1018525\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Not everyone sees the Supernal the same way, though, is the thing. It's always framed by culture and personal perspective. One of the big stepping stones for a Mage is when they start to realize that their own perspective informs their approach to the Supernal and what they see is merely their limited human mind trying to grasp the grandest cosmic Truths. To borrow a concept, the act of observation changes the outcome.<br /> <br /> Still, that aside, it must be remembered that there is a lot of colour and noise in the modern syncretic echoes, but the Mexica still saw the underworld as shrouded in darkness and shadows. You got closer to Mictlan by descending into lightless caves; getting as deep as you could, where all light and sound from the world is cut off. There's plenty of room to borrow shadows there.<br /> <br /> Don't be afraid to syncretize and use symbolism as you see fit. The point of Mage isn't to provide an authentic itinerary of mythology; it's to tune that symbolism to whatever feels right to you and your player(s). Sleeper versions are the flawed ones. If something in the stories doesn't make sense or work for you or your player(s), change it.<br /> <br /> Remember that the raucous celebration that is found in so many memento moris are reminder that you will die too, one day, and therefore you should live your life well. Some such reminders are sober, but many are incitements to laugh and celebrate. Party now, because it might be your last chance. The dead don't <i>get</i> to celebrate, so the living do it for them; the dead don't get pretty flowers, so the living gather them in offerings.<br /> <br /> There's also the reminder that life comes from death. Like Quetzlcoatl's battle with the Lord of Mictlan to get the bones that humanity sprang from, death is a source of renewal and regeneration in this symbolism. Your ancestors, who are dead, made you, who is alive. One day, you will die, but the things you make will live on. Rot gives way to new growth. Life is a transitory, temporary thing and you can't escape that, so you might as well accept it.<br /> <br /> The character's Mystery Play might honestly be an attempt to bring such life to the underworld; to descend into Mictlan and bring light and colour to the dead whose bodies are rotting and who are surrounded with spiders and owls and shrieking winds; for whom light and warmth are but distant memories and whose eternities are dark, cold, and still. They might hear whispers up from the ground of some departed friend or relative, weeping for the loss of the joys they remembered in life. When the curiosity is too much, they descend into the underworld with garlands of flowers and a basket full of the finest foods, and dare to cross the waters of that bleak reflection of Texcoco to the city of the damned. There, they face a thousand-thousand dead souls, but not simple ghosts or shades; these are skeletons and rotting corpses who look despondently on at the figure among them who, unlike them, still has life. They cannot hope to steal it, but some may lash out in jealous rage. At every step of the way, the dead beg and plead for the wandering soul to take them out of the darkness, or to deliver messages to family, or, more importantly, to give them the contents of their basket. Refusal might be risky or heartbreaking, but they've got to find their friend.<br /> <br /> The Mystery Play ends when the character finally finds their friend and offers the gifts they brought; garlands of flowers and fine foods. And when they give the gifts, colour blooms in the underworld. It spreads like a raging flame. Rotten flesh becomes whole again. Cold winds become warm breezes. The dead take part in a celebration, joyous that they are still, in a sense, alive; alive in the memories and lives of their descendants. Their own deaths are given colour and beauty by defining what their life was lived for. And at the heart of it all, there stands the spectre who gave them passage across the lake, demanding payment at last. The character gives the leaden coin that was in their pocket - a black thing, heavy with the grief that brought them into this dark place - and signs their name in the book of passage, before being directed to the way out: A long climb up the dead tree that sits at the heart of the city, its leaves now blooming into colour, up to where the ceiling of the great cave splits and the character may at last climb to the surface.<br /> <br /> They merge coated in dirt and dust and ash and their soul renewed by the knowledge that death need no longer be feared, so long as one's deeds remain. They are a Moros, now and forevermore, building their mansion in the afterlife with every work a brick.</div> </div> </div> </div>Just... damn <br /> <br /> Exactly the kind of inspiration I was looking for. Thank you","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11839-cofdave","name":"CofDave","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"31"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-12-17T12:43:14-08:00","id":1018553,"title":"","content":"Glad you enjoyed it.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2855-axelgear","name":"Axelgear","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2855_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1450"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-12-17T13:37:39-08:00","id":1018568,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Mrmdubois</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1018456-mexican-moros?p=1018531#post1018531\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Stygia is the realm that Moros draw their power from, and it is -an- afterlife, not -the- afterlife.</div> </div> </div> </div>Nitpicking:<br /> <br /> No, it's not. Stygia is a <i>perspective</i>, not a place. It's an afterlife in the sense that it's an <i>idea</i> of an afterlife, but it's <i>not</i> an afterlife in the sense that it's the place your ghost and/or soul goes after death. IMO, this is an important distinction. Stygia is the idea that death is a transitory process, a milestone along the road of change. The nature of the transition, the character of where you're going, etc.: those are things that Stygia incorporates, but isn't defined by.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>I call the Integrity-analogue the &quot;<a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/822559-trying-to-understand-the-morality-system?p=825008#post825008\" target=\"_blank\">subjective stat</a>&quot;.<br /> An explanation how to <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/913221-using-social-manuevering\" target=\"_blank\">use Social Manuevering</a>.<br /> Guanxi Explanations: <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/787518-the-mysterium-and-their-contribution-to-mage-society?p=787824#post787824\" target=\"_blank\">1</a>, <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/787518-the-mysterium-and-their-contribution-to-mage-society?p=788173#post788173\" target=\"_blank\">2</a>, <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/833863-mages-and-trading-favors?p=834531#post834531\" target=\"_blank\">3</a>.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2758-errol216","name":"Errol216","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2758_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1112"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-12-17T14:21:30-08:00","id":1018583,"title":"","content":"If we're sharing perspectives on Stygia, I'd describe it as, like all Supernal Realms, the source which gives those ideas substance. Where Stygia has sway over the world, those ideas are given form and reality. Stygia gives death and the material its form and shape. If an afterlife exists, it is sustained by Stygia (maybe), but Stygia itself isn't it.<br /> <br /> Stygia is why an afterlife can potentially exist, but it isn't one itself.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2855-axelgear","name":"Axelgear","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2855_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1450"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-12-17T14:23:36-08:00","id":1018586,"title":"","content":"You might look at Shadows of Mexico. It's not a mage book but it has some mage stuff in it, including at least one legacy, <a href=\"http://whitewolf.wikia.com/wiki/Dreamers_of_the_Black_Sun\" target=\"_blank\">the Dreamers of the Black Sun</a>. Big fan.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/886-nofather","name":"nofather","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar886_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11016"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-12-17T21:29:40-08:00","id":1018683,"title":"","content":"I recommend playing or watching a Let's Play of Grim Fandango. Obviously, it's not a Mage Game, but it should help you grasp the themes you want to explore.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/9102-whw","name":"WHW","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar9102_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2542"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-12-18T17:37:50-08:00","id":1018980,"title":"","content":"Watch the movie <b>The Book of Life.</b> Guillermo Del Toro says this is authentic modern day Mexican culture/mythos. It's a beautiful movie. It's awesome. Me and my daughters love watching for Halloween and just about every other month or so. Great music and all that jazz.<br /> <br /> Watch any of Guillermo Del Toro's early movies, too. They will give you a Mexican person's point of view on death and ghosts and vampires and things like that.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/346-troy","name":"Troy","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar346_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"293"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-12-18T18:37:17-08:00","id":1018988,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Troy</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1018456-mexican-moros?p=1018980#post1018980\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Watch the movie <b>The Book of Life.</b> Guillermo Del Toro says this is authentic modern day Mexican culture/mythos. It's a beautiful movie. It's awesome. Me and my daughters love watching for Halloween and just about every other month or so. Great music and all that jazz.<br /> <br /> Watch any of Guillermo Del Toro's early movies, too. They will give you a Mexican person's point of view on death and ghosts and vampires and things like that.</div> </div> </div> </div>Absolutely love Book of Life. I'll check out his earlier work, thank you","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11839-cofdave","name":"CofDave","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"31"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-12-19T05:05:22-08:00","id":1019169,"title":"","content":"I was about to write something inspiring and deep about mexican awakening but then i rralized that <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2855-axelgear\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar2855_2.png');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"2855\" data-vbnamecard=\"2855\">Axelgear</a> alrrady said it all<br /> <br /> Another thing i feel its good to add is take a look on the figure of the Santa Muerte (holy death) and remember that usually death ghosts and the concept of afterlife is something closer to them and an everyday recurrence. Death is A grim reaper but also a lover a deity and a friend.<br /> <br /> <br /> Also!, if u really want to get inspired on mexico and magic, u should REALLY take a look here <br /> <br /> <div class=\"b-bbcode b-bbcode__url b-bbcode__url--preview\"> <a href=\"https://youtu.be/YKVlMMbTI9s\" target=\"_blank\" class=\"urlimg_container\"> <img class=\"js-img-fallback\" src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/default/google-material/image_placeholder.svg\"> </a> <div class=\"urlinfo\"> <div class=\"title ellipsis\"> <a href=\"https://youtu.be/YKVlMMbTI9s\" target=\"_blank\"> </a> </div> <div class=\"url ellipsis\"> <a href=\"https://youtu.be/YKVlMMbTI9s\" target=\"_blank\"> https://youtu.be/YKVlMMbTI9s </a> </div> <div class=\"meta\"> </div> </div> </div>","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4247-lasombra","name":"Lasombra","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar4247_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"179"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-12-19T12:09:09-08:00","id":1019363,"title":"","content":"Santa Muerte is a pretty great thing to play with, especially if your player wants their character to become a thearch. She's venerated at large like a saint, and is more or less the patron saint of everyone whose life is rendered miserable by the ways of the world; the desperately poor, prostitutes, honest police, LGBTQ people... She is a protector from violence and cruelty.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2855-axelgear","name":"Axelgear","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2855_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1450"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}